      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\n**Certificate-level info:**\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1\n- Scheme: A\n- Member state: Slovakia\n- Issuing authority: Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM), Hviezdoslavova 1124/31, 974 01 Banská Bystrica, Slovakia; Person responsible: Peter Vook, Director\n- Date issued: The revision 1 is dated 25th May 2023 (signature date). Original issued 13th January 2023. Since this is Rev. 1, date_issued should be 2023-05-25.\n- Project number: TK-P05-85? That's a form number. Hmm. The project number could be 2022/ER010/SK1? Actually that's the type evaluation report number. TK-P05-85 is a template/form number. I'll omit project_number or use... Actually there's no explicit project number. Omit.\n- page_total: 12\n- oiml_issuer_id: not present explicitly.\n\n**Applicant:** UAB „Axioma Metering\", Veterinaru str. 52, Biruliskes, LT-54469 Kaunas region, Lithuania\n\n**Manufacturer:** same\n\n**Certified type:**\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"Qalcosonic W1\"] (also QALCOSONIC W1)\n- module_designation: \"Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\"\n- description: summary\n\n**Recommendation:**\n- id: R49, edition: 2013, scheme: A, accuracy_classes: [\"2\"]\n\n**Test reports:**\n- 2022/ER010/SK1, dated 2023-01-13, 17 pages, role: type_evaluation_report\n- Documentation file: \"Technical documentation file AXIOMA_Qalcosonic W1_00\", dated 2023-01-13, 114 pages, role: documentation_file\n\n**Revision history:**\n- Revision \"0\", 2023-01-13, \"Certificate first issued\"\n- Revision \"1\", 2023-05-25, \"Point 2.4 Software specification – supplemented specification for all DNs separately\"\n\n**Model family:** The cert covers DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32, DN40 with various Q3 and ratio variants. This is a complex family. Let me build the models.\n\nTab. 1 (DN15): Q3 = 1.6 with Q1: 0.020, 0.010, 0.0064, 0.005, 0.004 → ratios 80, 160, 250, 315, 400; and Q3 = 2.5 with Q1: 0.031, 0.0156, 0.010, 0.0062, 0.0031 → ratios 80, 160, 250, 400, 800.\n\nTab. 2 (DN20): Q3 = 2.5 with Q1: 0.031, 0.0156, 0.010, 0.0062 → ratios 80, 160, 250, 400; and Q3 = 4 with Q1: 0.050, 0.025, 0.016, 0.010, 0.005 → ratios 80, 160, 250, 400, 800.\n\nTab. 3 (DN25): Q3 = 6.3 with Q1: 0.079, 0.040, 0.252 (probably 0.0252, OCR error), 0.016 → ratios 80, 160, 250, 400; and Q3 = 10 with Q1: 0.125, 0.0625, 0.040, 0.025 → ratios 80, 160, 250, 400.\n\nTab. 4 (DN32): Q3 = 6.3 with Q1: 0.079, 0.040, 0.0252, 0.016 → ratios 80, 160, 250, 400; and Q3 = 10 with Q1: 0.125, 0.0625, 0.025 → ratios 80, 160, 400.\n\nTab. 5 (DN40): Q3 = 10 with Q1: 0.125, 0.0625, 0.040 → ratios 80, 160, 250; Q3 = 16 with Q1: 0.200, 0.100, 0.064, 0.040 → ratios 80, 160, 250, 400; Q3 = 25 with Q1: 0.3125, 0.156, 0.100, 0.0625 → ratios 80, 160, 250, 400.\n\nSo the family has many variants. How to structure model_id? Something like \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R=80\", etc. Let me enumerate:\n\nDN15:\n- Q3=1.6: R=80 (Q1=0.020), R=160 (Q1=0.010), R=250 (Q1=0.0064), R=315 (Q1=0.005), R=400 (Q1=0.004). Q4=2. Q2/Q1=1.6.\n- Q3=2.5: R=80 (Q1=0.031), R=160 (Q1=0.0156), R=250 (Q1=0.010), R=400 (Q1=0.0062), R=800 (Q1=0.0031). Q4=3.125.\n\nDN20:\n- Q3=2.5: R=80 (0.031), 160 (0.0156), 250 (0.010), 400 (0.0062). Q4=3.125.\n- Q3=4: R=80 (0.050), 160 (0.025), 250 (0.016), 400 (0.010), 800 (0.005). Q4=5.\n\nDN25:\n- Q3=6.3: R=80 (0.079), 160 (0.040), 250 (0.252→0.0252), 400 (0.016). Q4=7.875.\n- Q3=10: R=80 (0.125), 160 (0.0625), 250 (0.040), 400 (0.025). Q4=12.5.\n\nDN32:\n- Q3=6.3: R=80 (0.079), 160 (0.040), 250 (0.0252), 400 (0.016). Q4=7.875.\n- Q3=10: R=80 (0.125), 160 (0.0625), 400 (0.025). Q4=12.5.\n\nDN40:\n- Q3=10: R=80 (0.125), 160 (0.0625), 250 (0.040). Q4=12.5.\n- Q3=16: R=80 (0.200), 160 (0.100), 250 (0.064), 400 (0.040). Q4=20.0.\n- Q3=25: R=80 (0.3125), 160 (0.156), 250 (0.100), 400 (0.0625). Q4=31.25.\n\nTotal variants: 5+5+4+5+4+4+4+3+3+4+4 = 45. That's a lot. Model IDs like \"DN15 Q3=1.6 R=80\".\n\nThis is a lot of data but the schema asks to preserve it. I'll create matrix_tables for Tab.1–Tab.5 and populate model_level characteristics... 45 models × multiple attributes would be enormous in model_level. Maybe better: model_level entries keyed by model for q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio. But with 45 models that's 5 attributes × 45 values = 225 value objects. That's large but acceptable? Alternatively I could keep the matrix tables and use a more compact model definition.\n\nHmm, the schema says: \"model_level[]: attribute × model matrix\". With 45 models, that's heavy. But the instructions emphasize matrix detection as MOST IMPORTANT. The example R137 populated model_level per attribute. I should do the same but the volume is big. Let me think about a compromise: I'll populate model_family.models with 45 entries each carrying attributes {} — actually I can put the attributes directly in the model's attributes dict! The schema says models[] have \"model_id, label, attributes{}\". That's a natural place. Then model_level characteristics could be omitted or kept minimal to avoid duplication... But rule 4 says to populate both model_family.models AND characteristics.model_level for the R137 example.\n\nGiven the volume (45 variants), I think the best approach is:\n- model_family.models: 45 entries, each with attributes {nominal_dn, permanent_flowrate_q3, minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2, overload_flowrate_q4, ratio_q3_q1, ratio_q2_q1}\n- characteristics.model_level: per attribute across models — this would duplicate. Maybe I include the key ones: q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio_q3_q1. That's 5 × 45 = 225 value objects. It's verbose but correct per schema. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, to keep the output manageable, I could define model_id as DN+Q3+R combination and provide model_level for the varying attributes. Let me estimate JSON size: each value object ~60-80 bytes, 225 objects ≈ 15KB, plus models 45 × ~200 bytes ≈ 9KB. Total maybe 40-50KB. That's acceptable.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. Perhaps a cleaner approach: the axis of variation is (DN, Q3, R). I can create model_id = \"DN15-1.6-R80\" etc. In model_family.models each model gets attributes. For characteristics.model_level, per the schema it's \"attribute × model matrix\". I'll include it.\n\nHmm, but honestly, maybe there's a middle ground: since the schema allows attributes{} inside models[], and matrix_tables preserve the raw tables, I could put the full per-model data in model_family.models[].attributes and skip the giant model_level duplication, OR include model_level for the main flow attributes. The rules say for R137 \"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\". So the expected behavior is both. I'll do both to be safe. But 225 entries... Let me just do it programmatically in my head carefully.\n\nActually, let me count more carefully and decide. I'll define model ids:\n\nDN15:\n1. DN15_Q3=1.6_R80: q3=1.6, q1=0.020, q2=0.032, q4=2, r=80\n2. DN15_Q3=1.6_R160: q1=0.010, q2=0.016\n3. DN15_Q3=1.6_R250: q1=0.0064, q2=0.010\n4. DN15_Q3=1.6_R315: q1=0.005, q2=0.008\n5. DN15_Q3=1.6_R400: q1=0.004, q2=0.0064\n6. DN15_Q3=2.5_R80: q1=0.031, q2=0.050, q4=3.125\n7. DN15_Q3=2.5_R160: q1=0.0156, q2=0.025\n8. DN15_Q3=2.5_R250: q1=0.010, q2=0.016\n9. DN15_Q3=2.5_R400: q1=0.0062, q2=0.010\n10. DN15_Q3=2.5_R800: q1=0.0031, q2=0.005\n\nDN20:\n11. DN20_Q3=2.5_R80: q1=0.031, q2=0.05, q4=3.125\n12. DN20_Q3=2.5_R160: q1=0.0156, q2=0.025\n13. DN20_Q3=2.5_R250: q1=0.010, q2=0.016\n14. DN20_Q3=2.5_R400: q1=0.0062, q2=0.010\n15. DN20_Q3=4_R80: q1=0.050, q2=0.080, q4=5\n16. DN20_Q3=4_R160: q1=0.025, q2=0.040\n17. DN20_Q3=4_R250: q1=0.016, q2=0.026\n18. DN20_Q3=4_R400: q1=0.010, q2=0.016\n19. DN20_Q3=4_R800: q1=0.005, q2=0.008\n\nDN25:\n20. DN25_Q3=6.3_R80: q1=0.079, q2=0.126, q4=7.875\n21. DN25_Q3=6.3_R160: q1=0.040, q2=0.063\n22. DN25_Q3=6.3_R250: q1=0.0252 (source \"0,252\" — likely OCR error; ratio 250 → Q1 = 6.3/250 = 0.0252. So use 0.0252), q2=0.040\n23. DN25_Q3=6.3_R400: q1=0.016 (6.3/400 = 0.01575, they wrote 0,016), q2=0.026\n24. DN25_Q3=10_R80: q1=0.125, q2=0.200, q4=12.5\n25. DN25_Q3=10_R160: q1=0.0625, q2=0.100\n26. DN25_Q3=10_R250: q1=0.040, q2=0.064\n27. DN25_Q3=10_R400: q1=0.025, q2=0.040\n\nDN32:\n28. DN32_Q3=6.3_R80: q1=0.079, q2=0.126, q4=7.875\n29. DN32_Q3=6.3_R160: q1=0.040, q2=0.063\n30. DN32_Q3=6.3_R250: q1=0.0252, q2=0.040\n31. DN32_Q3=6.3_R400: q1=0.016, q2=0.026\n32. DN32_Q3=10_R80: q1=0.125, q2=0.200, q4=12.5\n33. DN32_Q3=10_R160: q1=0.0625, q2=0.100\n34. DN32_Q3=10_R400: q1=0.025, q2=0.040 (ratio 400 in table for DN32 Q3=10 — the ratios listed are 80, 160, 400)\n\nDN40:\n35. DN40_Q3=10_R80: q1=0.125, q2=0.200, q4=12.5\n36. DN40_Q3=10_R160: q1=0.0625, q2=0.100\n37. DN40_Q3=10_R250: q1=0.040, q2=0.064\n38. DN40_Q3=16_R80: q1=0.200, q2=0.320, q4=20.0\n39. DN40_Q3=16_R160: q1=0.100, q2=0.160\n40. DN40_Q3=16_R250: q1=0.064, q2=0.102\n41. DN40_Q3=16_R400: q1=0.040, q2=0.064\n42. DN40_Q3=25_R80: q1=0.3125, q2=0.500, q4=31.25\n43. DN40_Q3=25_R160: q1=0.156, q2=0.250\n44. DN40_Q3=25_R250: q1=0.100, q2=0.160\n45. DN40_Q3=25_R400: q1=0.0625, q2=0.100\n\n45 models. OK.\n\nFor model_level characteristics, attributes: permanent_flowrate_q3, minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2, overload_flowrate_q4, ratio_q3_q1. Units: m³/h for q1-q4, null for ratios.\n\nThat's 5 attributes × 45 models = 225 value entries. Plus 45 model entries in models[]. Hmm, that's a big JSON, but fine.\n\nActually, wait — maybe I should reconsider whether to include per-model attributes in models[] as well. The schema says models[]: \"model_id, label, attributes{}\". If I put attributes there, and also model_level, there's redundancy. For R137 example, the models[] entries were just {\"model_id\": \"G1.6\"} without attributes, and model_level carried the data. I'll follow that pattern: models[] with just model_id (and maybe label), and model_level with the matrices. Actually I could add nominal_dn to attributes since DN is a grouping... but DN is part of the model_id. Keep simple.\n\nBut actually, to reduce duplication, maybe I can skip model_level and keep attributes in models[]? The instructions show models[] without attributes in the example. I'll do the R137-style: matrix_tables + models[] (ids) + model_level (per attribute). I'll skip per-model attributes{} to match the example... Hmm, but with 45 models, the model_level becomes huge. Both representations are big. Let me just include both matrix_tables and model_level, and models with model_id only.\n\nActually, let me think about output size more carefully. The full JSON with:\n- matrix_tables: 5 tables, each with ~24 rows. Each row ~200 bytes → 5 × 24 × 200 = 24KB.\n- model_level: 5 attributes × 45 values × ~70 bytes = ~16KB.\n- models: 45 × 60 = 2.7KB.\n\nTotal ~45KB. That's a lot of writing but doable. Alternatively, drop matrix_tables? No — rule 4 says to include them. But maybe I can reduce: the type-level rows that are constant across all variants (ratio_q2_q1=1.6, MPE, scale interval, etc.) go to type_level. Only varying rows go to matrix_tables/model_level.\n\nLet me structure:\n\n**type_level characteristics** (constant across whole certificate):\n- accuracy_class: 2, unit null\n- mechanical_class: \"M1\"\n- climatic_class: {min:-15, max:70}, unit °C\n- electromagnetic_class: \"E2\"\n- environmental_classification: \"B/O\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- installation_position: \"Horizontal / Vertical\"\n- water_temperature_range: multiple ranges depending on temperature class — this varies by class config. Could be config_level with axis \"meter_temperature_class\": T30 → 0.1–30, T50 → 0.1–50, T30/70 → 30–70, T70 → 0.1–70. Good use of config_level!\n- max_permissible_error_upper: ±2% at Θ≤30°C; ±3% at Θ>30°C — could be two entries: mpe_upper_range: ±2 with condition, mpe_upper_range_above_30c: ±3. Or config_level with axis \"water_temperature\". I'll use config_level: attribute max_permissible_error_upper_flow_range, axis water_temperature_Θ, conditions \"Θ ≤ 30 °C\" → -2..2? Hmm, ±2 is a range -2 to 2. Let me express value as {min:-2, max:2}? Or \"±2\"? Rule: numbers not strings. ±2 → {min:-2, max:2} seems reasonable but unusual. Alternatively value 2 with the sign meaning tolerance. Hmm. I think for MPE, {min: -2, max: 2} in % is precise. Or keep as string \"±2\". The rule says numbers not strings, ranges become {min,max}. I'll use {min:-2,max:2}. Hmm, but actually ±2 for MPE conventionally means the error bound. I'll go with {min:-2, max:2}.\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider. \"Maximum permissible error ± 2\" — the value is 2, and it's a limit. I think {min:-2, max:2} is defensible and numeric. Let me do that.\n\n- mpe_lower_range: ±5 → {min:-5, max:5}, unit %\n- scale_interval: 0.001 m³\n- calculator_capacity: 999999.999 m³\n- ratio_q2_q1: 1.6 (dimensionless) — constant across all variants. Good, type_level.\n- battery: \"li-battery 3.6 V, life time 16 years\" — split: battery_type \"lithium\", battery_voltage 3.6 V, battery_life 16 years.\n- display_upper_line_digits: 9; display_lower_line_digits: 5.\n- max_working_pressure (MAP): 16 bar.\n- connection threads vary by DN — model_level or per-DN config. Threads: DN15 G¾, DN20 G1, DN25 G1¼, DN32 G1½, DN40 G2. Construction lengths vary by DN: DN15: 80,105,110,115,165,170; DN20: 105,110,130,165,190; DN25: 260; DN32: 260; DN40: 300.\n\nThese vary by DN, which is a model attribute. I could use config_level with axis \"nominal_diameter\". Since models are keyed by DN+Q3+R, and these are DN-dependent (not Q3/R-dependent), config_level with axis nominal_diameter makes sense. Values: {condition: \"DN15\", value: \"G 3/4\"}.\n\nActually, the schema for config_level values: \"{condition, value}\". With unit handled at attribute level presumably. Let me look at the schema:\n\n```\nconfig_level[]:                   # attribute × config-axis matrix\n    attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}\n```\n\nAnd rule 5: \"Values follow the same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\" OK so {condition, value, unit_symbol, unit_id?}.\n\nSo config_level entries:\n- attribute: connection_thread, axis: nominal_diameter, values: [{condition \"DN15\", value \"G 3/4\"}, {\"DN20\", \"G 1\"}, {\"DN25\", \"G 1 1/4\"}, {\"DN32\", \"G 1 1/2\"}, {\"DN40\", \"G 2\"}]\n- attribute: construction_length, axis: nominal_diameter, values: [{condition \"DN15\", value: [80,105,110,115,165,170]...}]. Hmm, value should be number, range, or string. Multiple options → maybe value as string list? Or I could make condition \"DN15\" and value as an array? The schema doesn't allow arrays directly. Options: separate value objects: {condition: \"DN15 L=80\", value: 80}... Or keep as string \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\". Rule says don't record lists as values in characteristics... that rule was about model matrices (multiple Emax values). For construction length, it's genuinely a list of available lengths. I'll express construction_length as config values with each length as separate condition? That's awkward. Better: value: \"80 / 105 / 110 / 115 / 165 / 170\" as string. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively treat it as: attribute construction_length, axis nominal_diameter, values [{condition DN15, value \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\"}]. Strings allowed. I'll do that with unit mm at entry level... The structured form wants value/unit separation. I could do value as string list and unit_symbol \"mm\". Acceptable.\n\nHmm, actually maybe simpler: I can put connection thread and construction length per-DN in config_level. Yes.\n\n- battery configuration varies by DN (from section 2.1): DN15/20/25/32: two \"AA\" internal batteries; some with + one \"A23\"; DN40: one \"D\" size; some with + one \"A23\". The text is contradictory (lists DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32 twice — presumably one line is for DN15-25 and other for DN32 or similar; it's an OCR/doc ambiguity). I'll capture battery_type config loosely or skip. Actually, I can record it in a note or as config_level: attribute battery_configuration, axis nominal_diameter, values: [{condition:\"DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\", value:\"2 × internal AA\"}, ...]. Given ambiguity, maybe record type_level battery: lithium 3.6V, life 16 years, and mention configurations in matrix_tables? I'll add config_level for battery_configuration with the four listed options. Hmm, it might be over-engineering. Let me include it — the cert explicitly lists battery options per DN.\n\nActually the listed options:\n- for DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32 two internal batteries \"AA\" size;\n- for DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32: two internal batteries \"AA\" size + one \"A23\";\n- for DN40 one internal battery \"D\" size;\n- for DN40 one internal battery \"D\" size + one \"A23\".\n\nI'll record as config_level battery_configuration with axis nominal_diameter and 4 conditions. Conditions like \"DN15/DN20/DN25/DN32\". Fine.\n\n**Water temperature range / temperature class**: config_level axis meter_temperature_class:\n- T30: {min:0.1, max:30} °C\n- T50: {min:0.1, max:50} °C\n- T30/70: {min:30, max:70} °C\n- T70: {min:0.1, max:70} °C\n\n**MPE upper**: config_level axis water_temperature:\n- \"Θ ≤ 30 °C\": {min:-2, max:2} %\n- \"Θ > 30 °C\": {min:-3, max:3} %\n\nMPE lower: type_level: {min:-5, max:5} %.\n\n**Pressure loss**: varies by DN and Q3:\n- Tab1 DN15: Q3=1.6 → 0.16 bar (well \"0,16\"); Q3=2.5 → 0.25 bar.\n\nWait, that's odd: pressure loss class 0,16 for Q3=1.6 and 0,25 for Q3=2.5? Typically Δp class is in bar (0.16 bar? Actually pressure loss classes are 0.063/0.04/0.025 bar...). R49 pressure-loss classes Δp: 0.063, 0.04, 0.025 bar? Hmm, actually R49-2014 classes: Δp 0.063/0.04/0.025/0.016/0.010/0.0063/0.004/0.0025 bar. But here values 0.16, 0.25, 0.63 bar appear. Whatever — extract as given. Actually wait, maybe it's Δp in bar: 0.16? That seems high but it's what the doc says. Hmm, or maybe those are in units of 0.1 bar? No, unit column says \"bar\". Extract as given.\n\n- Tab1 DN15: 0.16 (Q3=1.6), 0.25 (Q3=2.5)\n- Tab2 DN20: 0.16 (Q3=2.5), 0.25 (Q3=4)\n- Tab3 DN25: 0.25 (Q3=6.3), 0.63 (Q3=10)\n- Tab4 DN32: 0.16 (Q3=6.3), 0.25 (Q3=10)\n- Tab5 DN40: 0.16 (all Q3: 10, 16, 25)\n\nThis varies by (DN, Q3) → config_level with axis \"nominal_diameter/flowrate\"? Or include in matrix_tables rows and also model_level (since each model has a Q3). Since pressure loss depends on Q3 within a DN... Q3=2.5 appears in both DN15 and DN20 with different Δp (0.25 vs 0.16). So it's per (DN,Q3). I'll use config_level axis \"nominal_diameter + permanent_flowrate\" or just put it in matrix_tables only. Hmm.\n\nDecision: I'll put Δp in matrix_tables rows (per variant) AND as config_level with condition like \"DN15, Q3=1.6\". Actually, since the model set already distinguishes by (DN, Q3, R), and Δp is the same across R within a (DN,Q3) group, config_level with condition \"DN15 Q3=1.6 m³/h\" etc. works. Let me list:\n- DN15 Q3=1.6: 0.16\n- DN15 Q3=2.5: 0.25\n- DN20 Q3=2.5: 0.16\n- DN20 Q3=4: 0.25\n- DN25 Q3=6.3: 0.25\n- DN25 Q3=10: 0.63\n- DN32 Q3=6.3: 0.16\n- DN32 Q3=10: 0.25\n- DN40 (Q3=10,16,25): 0.16\n\nOK.\n\nSimilarly overload Q4 varies by (DN,Q3): 2, 3.125, 3.125, 5, 7.875, 12.5, 7.875, 12.5, 12.5, 20, 31.25. And Q3 varies by model; Q1, Q2 vary per R.\n\nNow, for model_level: attributes q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio_q3_q1 per model (45 models). And config_level for DN-level stuff (thread, length, Δp, battery). And matrix_tables preserving the 5 raw tables.\n\nAlternatively, to reduce model_level size, since q1/q2/q4/q3/ratio are all in matrix_tables rows, maybe model_level is redundant... but the rules explicitly ask for both. Let me include both. Actually, hold on — re-reading rule 4: \"EXTRACT as matrix_tables[] AND populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\". Yes, both.\n\nBut 45 models × 5 attributes... that's large. Alternative: maybe I can be smarter — put per-model attributes in models[].attributes (schema supports it!) and skip model_level. But the rule explicitly says populate model_level. Ugh. I'll include model_level. It's the \"most important\" rule, so I should follow it fully.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider the model_id granularity. Could I make model_id = \"DN15 Q3=1.6\" groups? No — Q1/Q2/R vary within that. The finest variant is (DN, Q3, R). 45 models it is.\n\nActually, hmm, let me reconsider: could I define models at (DN, Q3, R) with Q1/Q2 derived? Yes that's what I did. 45 entries.\n\nLet me now write out all data.\n\n**matrix_tables**: I'll create 5 tables, one per Tab. name \"Tab. 1 — DN15\" etc.\n\nColumns: nominal_diameter_dn, permanent_flowrate_q3, minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2, overload_flowrate_q4, ratio_q3_q1, ratio_q2_q1, connection_thread, construction_length, installation_position, water_temperature_range, meter_temperature_class, max_working_pressure, pressure_loss, mpe_upper_range, mpe_lower_range, scale_interval, calculator_capacity, accuracy_class, mechanical_class, climatic_class, electromagnetic_class, environmental_classification, flow_profile_sensitivity_class, battery.\n\nThat's too wide. Maybe matrix_tables should preserve just the varying columns: q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio_q3_q1, pressure_loss — with DN fixed per table. The description can note the rest is type-level. Let me define columns: [\"variant\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3_m3h\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1_m3h\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2_m3h\", \"overload_flowrate_q4_m3h\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"pressure_loss_bar\"]. Hmm, schema example had \"columns\" and \"column_units\" (the example included column_units even though the schema yaml doesn't list it; the R137 example output includes \"column_units\"). I'll include column_units.\n\nSchema yaml says: `name, columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]`. The example adds column_units. I'll include column_units — harmless and useful.\n\nRows per table: Tab1 has 10 variants, Tab2 9, Tab3 8, Tab4 7, Tab5 11. Total 45 rows. Each row: {\"variant\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6 R80\", \"q3\": 1.6, \"q1\": 0.020, \"q2\": 0.032, \"q4\": 2, \"ratio_q3_q1\": 80, \"pressure_loss\": 0.16}.\n\nWait Tab1 DN15 Q3=1.6: pressure loss 0.16? Let me re-check Tab.1: \"Pressure loss class ΔP | bar | colspan=5 0,16 | colspan=5 0,25\". Yes: Q3=1.6 group (5 cols) → 0.16; Q3=2.5 group (5 cols) → 0.25.\n\nTab2 DN20: Q3=2.5 (4 cols) → 0.16; Q3=4 (5 cols) → 0.25.\n\nTab3 DN25: Q3=6.3 (4 cols) → 0.25; Q3=10 (4 cols) → 0.63.\n\nTab4 DN32: Q3=6.3 (4 cols) → 0.16; Q3=10 (3 cols) → 0.25.\n\nTab5 DN40: single value 0.16 for all 11 variants.\n\nInteresting: DN25 has 0.25/0.63 while DN32 (same flows) has 0.16/0.25. Odd but as given. Also DN25 Q3=6.3 ratio 250 Q1 listed as \"0,252\" — clearly a typo for 0.0252 (matches Q3/R = 6.3/250 = 0.0252, and DN32 same cell shows 0.0252). Also Tab3 ratio row: 80, 160, 250, 400 — wait, Tab3 lists ratios: 80, 160, 250, 400 | 80, 160, 250, 400. And Q1 values 0.079, 0.040, 0.252, 0.016 for the first group. With ratio 250, Q1 = 0.0252. So \"0,252\" is 0.0252. I'll correct to 0.0252 (numeric). Should I note the OCR typo? I could add a footnote. I'll just correct it silently; maybe mention in description. Actually rule 8 says don't fabricate — correcting an obvious OCR error to the mathematically consistent value is reasonable. I'll use 0.0252.\n\nTab4 DN32 Q3=10: ratios 80, 160, 400 (no 250). Q1: 0.125, 0.0625, 0.025. Q2: 0.200, 0.100, 0.040. Good.\n\nTab5 DN40: ratios 80,160,250 | 80,160,250,400 | 80,160,250,400. Q1: 0.125,0.0625,0.040 | 0.200,0.100,0.064,0.040 | 0.3125,0.156,0.100,0.0625. Q2: 0.200,0.100,0.064 | 0.320,0.160,0.102,0.064 | 0.500,0.250,0.160,0.100.\n\nNote DN40 Q3=25 R160: Q1=0.156 (25/160 = 0.15625, they wrote 0.156). Fine.\n\nNow **models[]**: 45 entries with model_id like \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R80\". Or maybe include label \"QALCOSONIC W1 DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h, R80\". Adding labels for 45 models adds bulk; I'll include a concise label maybe. Actually schema: model_id, label, attributes{}. Label optional-ish (\"label\" listed). I'll include label as \"QALCOSONIC W1 DN15 Q3=1.6 R80\"? That's just repeating model_id. I'll skip label or set it null? \"Don't fabricate\" — omit label. Hmm, or include family_name: \"QALCOSONIC W1\". Yes family_name: \"QALCOSONIC W1\".\n\n**model_level**: \n- permanent_flowrate_q3 (m³/h): 45 values\n- minimum_flowrate_q1 (m³/h): 45 values\n- transitional_flowrate_q2 (m³/h): 45 values\n- overload_flowrate_q4 (m³/h): 45 values\n- ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless): 45 values\n- pressure_loss (bar): could be model_level too since it varies per model group. Or config_level. I'll put it in config_level keyed by DN+Q3 condition to avoid 45 more entries. Hmm, but is config_level appropriate? Rule 5 says config axes are configurations not models. Δp varies by (DN, Q3), which is a subset of model definition. Putting it in model_level would be consistent (each model gets its Δp). 45 entries. It adds bulk but keeps consistency. Actually... I'll put pressure_loss in config_level with conditions like \"DN15 Q3=1.6 m³/h\" — 9 entries. That's cleaner. Hmm, but mixing might confuse. Let me think about what's most useful for a downstream parser: model_level values reference models by model_id. If Δp is per-model-group, config_level with explicit conditions is clearer. I'll go config_level.\n\nAlso nominal_diameter itself: DN is embedded in model_id. Should nominal_diameter be a model_level attribute? It would be 45 identical-per-DN entries. Could use config_level axis nominal_diameter? No — DN is the model axis itself. I could add model_level attribute nominal_diameter_dn (mm) with 45 values. That's redundant with model_id but explicit. I'll include it — it helps grouping. Hmm, more bulk. Let me decide: include q3, q1, q2, q4, ratio, and dn. 6 attributes × 45 = 270 value objects. Plus models 45. It's big but let's go. Actually, let me drop dn from model_level since model_id starts with \"DN15_\" — parseable. Hmm. I think including it is safer for extraction completeness. I'll include it.\n\nActually, you know, let me reconsider total output size seriously. Each model_level value object like {\"model\":\"DN15_Q3=1.6_R80\",\"value\":1.6} ≈ 45 chars. 270 × 45 ≈ 12KB. Fine.\n\n**Components**: The water meter has flow sensor + calculator/indication device (compact, integrated). Section 2.1 describes essential parts. I can record components:\n- role \"flow_sensor\", description: plastic cylindrical brass body..., two ultrasonic transducers, measuring principle ultrasonic transit-time.\n- role \"calculator_and_indication_device\", type... LCD 2-line, 9-digit upper, 5-digit lower, non-replaceable lithium batteries.\n\ntype_designations: none specific. I'll include components with descriptions.\n\n**Software versions**: software versions 1.03 (checksum C983, DN15/20/25/32) and 2.02 (CD00, DN40). Where to put? Could be config_level: attribute software_version_checksum, axis nominal_diameter: DN15/20/25/32 → {version 1.03, checksum C983}; DN40 → {2.02, CD00}. Or matrix_tables. Or characteristics type_level as string? I'll add config_level entries: attribute \"firmware_version\", axis \"nominal_diameter\", values [{condition \"DN15/DN20/DN25/DN32\", value \"1.03\"}, {condition \"DN40\", value \"2.02\"}] and attribute \"firmware_checksum\", values [{..., \"C983\"}, {..., \"CD00\"}]. Good.\n\n**Battery config**: config_level battery_configuration.\n\n**Footnotes**: There are no numbered footnotes in this cert. Omit.\n\n**Test reports**:\n- {id: \"2022/ER010/SK1\", date: \"2023-01-13\", pages: 17, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"}\n- {id: \"Technical documentation file AXIOMA_Qalcosonic W1_00\", date: \"2023-01-13\", pages: 114, role: \"documentation_file\"}\n\n**Certificate**: number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02\" with revision \"Rev. 1\". The number field: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1\" as printed. I'll put number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02\", and revision info in revision_history. Hmm, the printed number includes \"Rev. 1\". I'll use number \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02\" and maybe note revision... The schema has certificate.number; revision_history captures revs. I'll use the full printed form? The rule example for revision_history says revision as string. For certificate.number, I'll use \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02\" — cleaner; but the doc header says \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1\". I could include number: \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02 Rev. 1\" verbatim. Hmm. I'll go with \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02\" and let revision_history convey Rev 0/1. Actually, to be faithful, maybe number should be the canonical certificate number without revision. I'll do that.\n\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: not present. TK-P05-85 is a form code. Omit.\n- page_total: 12\n- member_state: \"Slovakia\"\n- date_issued: \"2023-05-25\" (Rev 1 signature date). \n- oiml_issuer_id: not present; omit.\n\n**issuing_authority**: name \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\", address_lines [\"Hviezdoslavova 1124/31\", \"974 01 Banská Bystrica\", \"Slovakia\"], person_responsible \"Peter Vook\", person_title \"Director\". phone/email/website: none. oiml_issuer_id: omit.\n\n**certified_type**:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"Qalcosonic W1\", \"QALCOSONIC W1\"] — hmm, duplicates. Just [\"Qalcosonic W1\"]. Maybe normalize to \"QALCOSONIC W1\". I'll use \"Qalcosonic W1\" (as on page 1) — or both? One designation; I'll use \"Qalcosonic W1\".\n- module_designation: \"Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\". Hmm, \"module designation\" here is the module per OIML-CS (the certified module/type). The field in schema is module_designation with examples like \"Analog load cell\". I'll put \"Ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device\" as given.\n- description: concise summary of section 1.\n\n**recommendation**: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2013, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [\"2\"]}. edition as int 2013. accuracy_classes [\"2\"].\n\nNow let me also double check climatic class: \"-15 to +70\" °C → {min:-15, max:70}.\n\nConstruction lengths:\n- DN15: 80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170 mm\n- DN20: 105, 110, 130, 165, 190 mm\n- DN25: 260 mm\n- DN32: 260 mm\n- DN40: 300 mm\n\nDisplay: upper line 9 digits, range 999999.999 m³; lower line 5 digits flow rate m³/h.\n\nBattery: \"li-battery 3,6 V, life time 16 years\" → battery_chemistry \"lithium\", battery_voltage 3.6 V, battery_life 16 years.\n\nMPE rows: I'll do config_level for upper, type_level for lower.\n\nNow scale: \"Scale interval 0,001 m³\" → 0.001 m³. \"Capacity of calculator 999999,999 m³\" → 999999.999 m³.\n\nAccuracy class 2, mechanical M1, electromagnetic E2, environmental B/O, flow profile U0D0.\n\nInstallation position: Horizontal/Vertical — string value.\n\nConnection thread values: \"G ¾\" → \"G 3/4\"? I'll write \"G 3/4\", \"G 1\", \"G 1 1/4\", \"G 1 1/2\", \"G 2\".\n\nNow the type_designations for certified_type: \"Qalcosonic W1\". Good.\n\nLet me now also decide about model naming convention. I'll use e.g. \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R80\". Maybe clearer: \"DN15 Q3=1.6 R80\". Spaces in ids are fine. I'll use underscore-free readable: \"DN15-Q3=1.6-R80\"? The R60 example used \"Emax=25t\". I'll use \"DN15_Q3=1.6m³/h_R80\"? Keep simpler: \"DN15_Q3=1.6_R80\".\n\nHmm wait, R values: for DN15 Q3=1.6: 80,160,250,315,400. Q1: 0.020,0.010,0.0064,0.005,0.004. Check: 1.6/80=0.02 ✓; 1.6/160=0.01 ✓; 1.6/250=0.0064 ✓; 1.6/315=0.00508→0.005 ✓; 1.6/400=0.004 ✓. Q2 = 1.6×Q1: 0.032, 0.016, 0.010(0.01024), 0.008, 0.0064 ✓ matches.\n\nDN15 Q3=2.5: Q1 0.031,0.0156,0.010,0.0062,0.0031 for R 80,160,250,400,800. 2.5/80=0.03125→0.031 ✓; /160=0.015625→0.0156 ✓; /250=0.01 ✓; /400=0.00625→0.0062 ✓; /800=0.003125→0.0031 ✓. Q2: 0.050,0.025,0.016,0.010,0.005 ✓.\n\nDN20 Q3=2.5 (R 80,160,250,400): Q1 0.031,0.0156,0.010,0.0062; Q2 0.05,0.025,0.016,0.010. Q4=3.125.\nDN20 Q3=4 (R 80,160,250,400,800): Q1 0.050,0.025,0.016,0.010,0.005; Q2 0.080,0.040,0.026,0.016,0.008. Q4=5.\n\nDN25 Q3=6.3 (R 80,160,250,400): Q1 0.079,0.040,0.0252,0.016; Q2 0.126,0.063,0.040,0.026. Q4=7.875.\nDN25 Q3=10 (R 80,160,250,400): Q1 0.125,0.0625,0.040,0.025; Q2 0.200,0.100,0.064,0.040. Q4=12.5.\n\nDN32 Q3=6.3 (R 80,160,250,400): Q1 0.079,0.040,0.0252,0.016; Q2 0.126,0.063,0.040,0.026. Q4=7.875.\nDN32 Q3=10 (R 80,160,400): Q1 0.125,0.0625,0.025; Q2 0.200,0.100,0.040. Q4=12.5.\n\nDN40 Q3=10 (R 80,160,250): Q1 0.125,0.0625,0.040; Q2 0.200,0.100,0.064. Q4=12.5.\nDN40 Q3=16 (R 80,160,250,400): Q1 0.200,0.100,0.064,0.040; Q2 0.320,0.160,0.102,0.064. Q4=20.0.\nDN40 Q3=25 (R 80,160,250,400): Q1 0.3125,0.156,0.100,0.0625; Q2 0.500,0.250,0.160,0.100. Q4=31.25.\n\nTotal models: 5+5+4+5+4+4+4+3+3+4+4 = 45. ✓.\n\nNow decide final JSON structure. Let me draft:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-SK1-2023.02\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 12,\n    \"member_state\": \"Slovakia\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2023-05-25\"\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Slovak Legal Metrology (SLM)\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Hviezdoslavova 1124/31\", \"974 01 Banská Bystrica\", \"Slovakia\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Peter Vook\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Director\"\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"UAB „Axioma Metering“\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Veterinaru str. 52, Biruliskes\", \"LT-54469 Kaunas region\", \"Lithuania\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"manufacturers\": [\n    {\n      \"name\": \"UAB „Axioma Metering“\",\n      \"address_lines\": [\"Veterinaru str. 52, Biruliskes\", \"LT-54469 Kaunas region\", \"Lithuania\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"Qalcosonic W1\"],\n    \"module_designation\": \"Ultrasonic water meters with electronic indication device\",\n    \"description\": \"Compact ultrasonic water meter with electronic indication device for measurement of clean water in residential use, non-resettable. Measurement based on ultrasonic bidirectional transit-time principle. Can be installed in horizontal and vertical positions; not designed to measure reverse flow.\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": \"QALCOSONIC W1\",\n    \"models\": [ ... 45 ... ]\n  },\n  \"components\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"flow_sensor\",\n      \"type_designations\": [],\n      \"description\": \"Plastic cylindrical brass body with inlet and outlet firmly connected with the plastic housing for the calculator; inner plastic element with two mirrors (sound path); two ultrasonic transducers on the upside of the cylindrical plastic body.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"role\": \"calculator_and_indication_device\",\n      \"type_designations\": [],\n      \"description\": \"Plastic housing with indication device directly mounted on the flow sensor; three PCBs and one PCB with calculator; electronic LCD display with 2 lines (upper line 9 digits, indication range 999999.999 m³; bottom line 5 digits for current flow rate in m³/h); non-replaceable lithium batteries, life 16 years.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nHmm, \"alternatives\" field only when alternatives offered; omit there. Should type_designations be empty arrays or omitted? Rule 8: omit if not present. I'll omit type_designations for components.\n\nNon-essential parts: antenna; non-return valve (optional). Could add as components? They're optional accessories. I'll mention antenna in description or as component with role \"accessory\". Maybe add components: {role: \"antenna\", ...} and {role: \"non-return_valve\", description: \"optional\"}. I'll include them briefly.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\ntype_level:\n- accuracy_class: {value: 2, unit_symbol: null}\n- mechanical_class: {value: \"M1\"}\n- climatic_class: {value: {min:-15, max:70}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- electromagnetic_class: {value: \"E2\"}\n- environmental_classification: {value: \"B/O\"}\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0D0\"}\n- installation_position: {value: \"Horizontal / Vertical\"}\n- ratio_q2_q1: {value: 1.6}\n- max_working_pressure_map: {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n- max_permissible_error_lower_flow_rates: {value: {min:-5,max:5}, unit_symbol: \"%\"}\n- scale_interval: {value: 0.001, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n- calculator_capacity: {value: 999999.999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n- display_volume_indication_digits: {value: 9}\n- display_volume_indication_range: {value: 999999.999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}\n- display_flow_rate_digits: {value: 5}\n- battery_chemistry: {value: \"lithium\"}\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_life: {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"years\"}\n\nmodel_level (6 attributes × 45).\n\nconfig_level:\n- water_temperature_range by meter_temperature_class (axis meter_temperature_class)\n- mpe upper by water temperature\n- connection_thread by DN\n- construction_length by DN\n- pressure_loss by DN+Q3\n- battery_configuration by DN\n- firmware version/checksum by DN\n\nLet me write config_level entries:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"water_temperature_range\",\n  \"axis\": \"meter_temperature_class\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"T30\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 30}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"T50\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"T30/70\", \"value\": {\"min\": 30, \"max\": 70}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"T70\", \"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 70}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"max_permissible_error_upper_flow_rates\",\n  \"axis\": \"water_temperature\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"Θ ≤ 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -2, \"max\": 2}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Θ > 30 °C\", \"value\": {\"min\": -3, \"max\": 3}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nΘ is a Greek symbol — fine (not LaTeX). Maybe write \"T ≤ 30 °C\"? The cert uses Θ. Keep \"Θ ≤ 30 °C\".\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"connection_thread\",\n  \"axis\": \"nominal_diameter\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN15\", \"value\": \"G 3/4\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN20\", \"value\": \"G 1\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN25\", \"value\": \"G 1 1/4\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN32\", \"value\": \"G 1 1/2\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN40\", \"value\": \"G 2\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"construction_length\",\n  \"axis\": \"nominal_diameter\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN15\", \"value\": [80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170], \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, array value — schema says value is number|{min,max}|string. An array isn't allowed strictly. Use string \"80 / 105 / 110 / 115 / 165 / 170\". I'll use string with commas: \"80, 105, 110, 115, 165, 170\". OK.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss\",\n  \"axis\": \"nominal_diameter + permanent_flowrate_q3\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6 m³/h\", \"value\": 0.16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN15 Q3=2.5 m³/h\", \"value\": 0.25, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN20 Q3=2.5 m³/h\", \"value\": 0.16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN20 Q3=4 m³/h\", \"value\": 0.25, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN25 Q3=6.3 m³/h\", \"value\": 0.25, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN25 Q3=10 m³/h\", \"value\": 0.63, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN32 Q3=6.3 m³/h\", \"value\": 0.16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN32 Q3=10 m³/h\", \"value\": 0.25, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN40 (Q3=10/16/25 m³/h)\", \"value\": 0.16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nbattery_configuration:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"battery_configuration\",\n  \"axis\": \"nominal_diameter\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\", \"value\": \"2 × internal battery \\\"AA\\\"\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\", \"value\": \"2 × internal battery \\\"AA\\\" + 1 × \\\"A23\\\"\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN40\", \"value\": \"1 × internal battery \\\"D\\\"\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN40\", \"value\": \"1 × internal battery \\\"D\\\" + 1 × \\\"A23\\\"\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nDuplicate conditions — allowed? It reflects doc ambiguity. Maybe merge with \"/\" ... I'll keep as is; or combine: \"DN15/DN20/DN25/DN32\" twice. It's fine.\n\nfirmware:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"firmware_version\",\n  \"axis\": \"nominal_diameter\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\", \"value\": \"1.03\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN40\", \"value\": \"2.02\"}\n  ]\n},\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"firmware_checksum\",\n  \"axis\": \"nominal_diameter\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN15, DN20, DN25, DN32\", \"value\": \"C983\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN40\", \"value\": \"CD00\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nmatrix_tables: 5 entries. Columns: [\"variant\", \"permanent_flowrate_q3\", \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", \"transitional_flowrate_q2\", \"overload_flowrate_q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\", \"pressure_loss\"]. Units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null, \"bar\"].\n\nRows for Tab.1 (DN15):\n1. {\"variant\": \"DN15 Q3=1.6 R80\", \"q3\": 1.6, \"q1\": 0.020, \"q2\": 0.032, \"q4\": 2, \"r\": 80, \"dp\": 0.16}\n2. R160: 0.010, 0.016\n3. R250: 0.0064, 0.010\n4. R315: 0.005, 0.008\n5. R400: 0.004, 0.0064\n6. Q3=2.5 R80: 0.031, 0.050, q4 3.125, dp 0.25\n7. R160: 0.0156, 0.025\n8. R250: 0.010, 0.016\n9. R400: 0.0062, 0.010\n10. R800: 0.0031, 0.005\n\nTab.2 (DN20):\n1. Q3=2.5 R80: q1 0.031, q2 0.05, q4 3.125, dp 0.16\n2. R160: 0.0156, 0.025\n3. R250: 0.010, 0.016\n4. R400: 0.0062, 0.010\n5. Q3=4 R80: 0.050, 0.080, q4 5, dp 0.25\n6. R160: 0.025, 0.040\n7. R250: 0.016, 0.026\n8. R400: 0.010, 0.016\n9. R800: 0.005, 0.008\n\nTab.3 (DN25):\n1. Q3=6.3 R80: 0.079, 0.126, q4 7.875, dp 0.25\n2. R160: 0.040, 0.063\n3. R250: 0.0252, 0.040\n4. R400: 0.016, 0.026\n5. Q3=10 R80: 0.125, 0.200, q4 12.5, dp 0.63\n6. R160: 0.0625, 0.100\n7. R250: 0.040, 0.064\n8. R400: 0.025, 0.040\n\nTab.4 (DN32):\n1. Q3=6.3 R80: 0.079, 0.126,