eter_dn (mm)**:\nDN15: models 1-10 → 15\nDN20: models 11-19 → 20\nDN25: models 20-27 → 25\nDN32: models 28-34 → 32\nDN40: models 35-45 → 40\n\n**q3 (m³/h)**:\n1-5: 1.6; 6-10: 2.5; 11-14: 2.5; 15-19: 4; 20-23: 6.3; 24-27: 10; 28-31: 6.3; 32-34: 10; 35-37: 10; 38-41: 16; 42-45: 25.\n\n**q1 (m³/h)**:\n1: 0.02; 2: 0.01; 3: 0.0064; 4: 0.005; 5: 0.004; 6: 0.031; 7: 0.0156; 8: 0.01; 9: 0.0062; 10: 0.0031;\n11: 0.031; 12: 0.0156; 13: 0.01; 14: 0.0062; 15: 0.05; 16: 0.025; 17: 0.016; 18: 0.01; 19: 0.005;\n20: 0.079; 21: 0.04; 22: 0.0252; 23: 0.016; 24: 0.125; 25: 0.0625; 26: 0.04; 27: 0.025;\n28: 0.079; 29: 0.04; 30: 0.0252; 31: 0.016; 32: 0.125; 33: 0.0625; 34: 0.025;\n35: 0.125; 36: 0.0625; 37: 0.04; 38: 0.2; 39: 0.1; 40: 0.064; 41: 0.04; 42: 0.3125; 43: 0.156; 44: 0.1; 45: 0.0625.\n\n**q2 (m³/h)**:\n1: 0.032; 2: 0.016; 3: 0.01; 4: 0.008; 5: 0.0064; 6: 0.05; 7: 0.025; 8: 0.016; 9: 0.01; 10: 0.005;\n11: 0.05; 12: 0.025; 13: 0.016; 14: 0.01; 15: 0.08; 16: 0.04; 17: 0.026; 18: 0.016; 19: 0.008;\n20: 0.126; 21: 0.063; 22: 0.04; 23: 0.026; 24: 0.2; 25: 0.1; 26: 0.064; 27: 0.04;\n28: 0.126; 29: 0.063; 30: 0.04; 31: 0.026; 32: 0.2; 33: 0.1; 34: 0.04;\n35: 0.2; 36: 0.1; 37: 0.064; 38: 0.32; 39: 0.16; 40: 0.102; 41: 0.064; 42: 0.5; 43: 0.25; 44: 0.16; 45: 0.1.\n\n**q4 (m³/h)**:\n1-5: 2; 6-10: 3.125; 11-14: 3.125; 15-19: 5; 20-23: 7.875; 24-27: 12.5; 28-31: 7.875; 32-34: 12.5; 35-37: 12.5; 38-41: 20; 42-45: 31.25.\n\n**ratio_q3_q1**:\n1-5: 80,160,250,315,400; 6-10: 80,160,250,400,800; 11-14: 80,160,250,400; 15-19: 80,160,250,400,800; 20-23: 80,160,250,400; 24-27: 80,160,250,400; 28-31: 80,160,250,400; 32-34: 80,160,400; 35-37: 80,160,250; 38-41: 80,160,250,400; 42-45: 80,160,250,400.\n\nOK. Now writing all this out. It's long but let's do it.\n\nAlso decide: should model_level include nominal_diameter_dn? I said yes. It'll be 45 more entries. Total model_level value objects: 6×45 = 270. OK.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider dropping dn from model_level to save space since every model_id contains it and matrix_tables... no, matrix_tables don't have a dn column per row (DN fixed per table). The DN is in variant string. Including nominal_diameter_dn as model_level attribute makes each model's DN explicit without string parsing. Keep it.\n\nAlso should I include attributes{} in each models[] entry? To avoid double duplication I'll leave models[] as {\"model_id\": \"...\"} only, matching the R137 example pattern.\n\nNow revision_history:\n```json\n[\n  {\"revision\": \"0\", \"date\": \"2023-01-13\", \"changes\": \"Certificate first issued\"},\n  {\"revision\": \"1\", \"date\": \"2023-05-25\", \"changes\": \"Point 2.4 Software specification – supplemented specification for all DNs separately\"}\n]\n```\n\ntest_reports:\n```json\n[\n  {\"id\": \"2022/ER010/SK1\", \"date\": \"2023-01-13\", \"pages\": 17, \"role\": \"type_evaluation_report\"},\n  {\"id\": \"Technical documentation file AXIOMA_Qalcosonic W1_00\", \"date\": \"2023-01-13\", \"pages\": 114, \"role\": \"documentation_file\"}\n]\n```\n\nfootnotes: none → omit.\n\nNow certificate.date_issued: The rev 0 was issued 2023-01-13; the current certificate (Rev 1) signed 2023-05-25. I'll use \"2023-05-25\".\n\nAlso accuracy class in recommendation: \"For accuracy class (if applicable): 2\" → accuracy_classes: [\"2\"] or [2]? Schema shows accuracy_classes[] — I'll use [\"2\"] as string since class designations are labels. For R49 class 2 (meter accuracy class). Use [\"2\"].\n\nCharacteristics type_level — attribute naming canonical:\n- accuracy_class\n- mechanical_class\n- climatic_class → hmm \"climatic_class\" value -15 to +70 °C.\n- electromagnetic_class\n- environmental_classification\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class\n- installation_position\n- ratio_q2_q1\n- maximum_working_pressure (MAP) → \"max_working_pressure_map\"\n- maximum_permissible_error_lower_flow_rates → maybe \"mpe_lower_flow_range\". I'll use \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_range\".\n- scale_interval\n- calculator_capacity → \"calculator_capacity\" (m³)\n- display_digits_volume_line → maybe put display details in components. I'll put \"display_upper_line_digits\": 9 and \"display_lower_line_digits\": 5? These are component characteristics really. I'll include them in the component description only, to keep type_level focused. Hmm, but structured is better. I'll add them to components as characteristics? The schema components have \"characteristics{}\". I can add characteristics to the calculator component:\n  \"characteristics\": {\"display_upper_line_digits\": {value: 9}, \"display_lower_line_digits\": {value: 5}, \"display_volume_range\": {value: 999999.999, unit_symbol: \"m³\"}, \"battery_life\": {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"years\"}, \"battery_chemistry\": {value: \"lithium\"}, \"battery_voltage\": {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}}\n  \n  That's neat. And keep type_level battery entries too? Redundant. I'll put battery info in type_level (it's in Tab tables as a type attribute) and display digits in component characteristics. Slight redundancy is OK.\n\nLet me finalize type_level:\n\n```json\n\"type_level\": {\n  \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": 2},\n  \"mechanical_class\": {\"value\": \"M1\"},\n  \"climatic_class\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -15, \"max\": 70}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n  \"electromagnetic_class\": {\"value\": \"E2\"},\n  \"environmental_classification\": {\"value\": \"B/O\"},\n  \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": {\"value\": \"U0D0\"},\n  \"installation_position\": {\"value\": \"Horizontal / Vertical\"},\n  \"ratio_q2_q1\": {\"value\": 1.6},\n  \"max_working_pressure_map\": {\"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"},\n  \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -5, \"max\": 5}, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n  \"scale_interval\": {\"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n  \"calculator_capacity\": {\"value\": 999999.999, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n  \"battery_chemistry\": {\"value\": \"lithium\"},\n  \"battery_voltage\": {\"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol